# The program's three edges, round-tripped (§1.11, panel 036).
#
# `read_file`, `write_file`, `args` and `exit` are written in Heroes, in
# `archive/bootstrap-rs/heroes/src/library/source.hero`, over `extern` declarations against
# `hero_os.h` — the same door SQLite and libm come through. So this case is not
# only about file I/O: it is the proof that Tier 2 reaches the operating system
# without a single new built-in and without a second composition form in the
# emitter.
#
# The path is relative, and that is the harness *fixed*: the three configurations
# used to run the binary from two different working directories (the crate's, and
# the repository root), so a relative path was a different file in each — measured,
# and the first version of this case failed on exactly that. It was `/tmp` until
# 2026-08-14, which was true of both platforms the CI covered and false of the
# third: Windows has no `/tmp`. `build/` is this project's own directory, created
# by `heroes doctor` and by every compile, and it exists wherever the compiler
# does — a fact about the tool rather than about the operating system.
# Bulk regression case.

constant PATH: str
    "build/heroes-golden-edges.txt"

function main()
    match write_file(path: PATH, text: "one\ntwo\nthree\n")
        .ok _  => print("wrote it")
        .err e => print("write failed: ", e.code)

    match read_file(PATH)
        .ok text => print("read ", len(text), " bytes")
        .err e   => print("read failed: ", e.code)

    # The error side, which is the half a happy-path test never reaches. The
    # code is a stable snake_case string (§4.6), so it can be asserted.
    match read_file("build/heroes-no-such-file-at-all")
        .ok _  => print("unexpected")
        .err e => print("missing: ", e.code)

    # `args()` gives what follows the program's name, and the golden harness
    # runs the binary with none — the off-by-one answered once, in the runtime.
    print("arguments: ", len(args()))
